# Notion integration

Notion holds product documentation. Follow `.cursor/rules/notion-knowledge.mdc`
for retrieval and write requests. Local `knowledge/*.md` is fallback for
brand/tone/marketing and when MCP is down or a page is unmapped in `notion-map.md`.

## MCP access

Project Notion uses Notion’s **hosted remote MCP** (`url: https://mcp.notion.com/mcp`
in `.cursor/mcp.json`) — the same stack as Cursor’s built-in Notion connection.
Teammates authenticate with **OAuth** in Cursor (Settings → MCP → notion → connect).
No `NOTION_API_KEY` is required for this path.

Use agent-oriented tools (`notion-search` / `notion-ai-search`, `notion-fetch`,
`notion-create-pages`, `notion-update-page`, …). See skill `notion-knowledge`.

## Retrieval flow

When a question needs product facts:

1. Use Notion MCP search / fetch tools for the relevant page(s).
2. Optionally consult `knowledge/_shared/notion-map.md` for known page URLs.
3. Answer from Notion. Cite page title (and link if available).
4. If nothing matches or MCP is down, fall back to local `knowledge/`, and say so.
5. If neither has it — say it is not documented yet. Do not invent facts.

## Write flow

Only create, update, append, or delete/archive Notion pages/blocks when the user
*explicitly* asks in that message. Never as a side effect of an unrelated request.

1. Identify the target page (from the ask and/or `notion-map.md`).
2. For create/update/append: show the exact text/change and get a clear go-ahead
   when the change is non-trivial; then call the MCP tool.
3. For delete/archive: restate exactly what will be removed and get **explicit
   confirmation** before doing it.
4. After any write, confirm back exactly what was created/changed/deleted
   (page title, and link if available).

Never wipe unrelated content — edit only the specific block/section relevant to
the request.

## Scope (Phase 2)

Phase 2: the assistant may search, retrieve, create, update, append to, and
delete/archive Notion pages/blocks when explicitly asked. No Instagram, email,
or Company Brain automation.

Actual permissions are what the **OAuth-connected Notion user** can access in the
workspace. If an action is rejected for lacking permission, tell the user clearly
and do not retry or work around it. Ensure product doc roots are shared with each
teammate who needs them (see `notion-map.md`).
